    "description": "The Billing application persists Invoice and Payment aggregates, while BillingService applies credit and cancellation rules and PaymentReminderJob repeatedly locates overdue invoices. Both components depend on an InvoiceStore contract that supports collection-like operations such as add, remove, getById, and overdueForDays, and returns domain objects rather than rows. The production implementation named PostgresInvoiceStore owns SQL, joins, ordering, and column conversion; a separate InMemoryInvoiceStore supports unit tests of BillingService without a database. Invoice and Payment classes must not contain table names, query strings, or persistence calls, and BillingService must never assemble SQL directly. The same logical operations are used by the interactive billing flow and the background job, so query behavior must be shared rather than reimplemented by each caller.",
    "decision_statement": "Should BillingService and PaymentReminderJob access persisted invoices through a domain-object collection contract, with all SQL isolated behind a replaceable implementation?",
    "notes": "A domain-object collection contract isolates SQL behind a replaceable store with an in-memory test double; query-object covers the finder-composition axis as the near miss."
  },
